{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_15",
      "quote": "This systematic review is the first to describe the prevalence of hepatitis B amongst refugees, asylum seekers and IDPs in LMIC. This topic is of public health relevance as LMIC have the highest burden of hepatitis B, and host the most refugees, but also face health system challenges and constrained resources. Refugees are a highly diverse, mobile population and have increased risk of poorer health outcomes and barriers in access to health care. Though few studies on this topic exist, the available evidence suggests a high prevalence amongst refugees in LMIC, with wide variation between and within countries. ... The available literature is scarce and of low quality, and further detailed studies are needed to further characterize the prevalence and risk factors for HBV amongst refugees in LMIC and inform robust and equitable health responses.",
      "reason": "Describes existing knowledge (LMIC burden, diversity and vulnerabilities of refugees) and highlights uncertainties (few studies, scarce and low-quality evidence, wide variation) indicating current state and gaps."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_15",
      "quote": "This systematic review is the first to describe the prevalence of hepatitis B amongst refugees, asylum seekers and IDPs in LMIC. This topic is of public health relevance as LMIC have the highest burden of hepatitis B, and host the most refugees, but also face health system challenges and constrained resources. ... The limited high-­quality evidence about prevalence and risk factors for hepatitis B amongst refugees in LMIC highlights the need for further characterization to inform appropriate public health responses, noting that services should adopt a human rights approach and be tailored to the circumstances of local refugee populations-­ not a 'one size fits all' approach.",
      "reason": "Explains importance/public-health relevance and explicitly states the need for further characterization and tailored public health responses, justifying conducting the review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_15",
      "quote": "Previous literature about HBV amongst refugees has mostly reported outcomes amongst those in high-­ income countries. A systematic review of HBV by Rossi et al. identified an overall prevalence of 7.2% amongst refugees and migrants arriving in low-­ endemic regions, and a higher risk of HBV amongst refugees compared with migrants (OR 1.42, 95% CI 1.03-­ 2.43). 21 ... No previous studies have assessed the prevalence of HBV amongst refugees in LMIC, where most of the global population of refugees reside.",
      "reason": "Cites existing systematic reviews (Rossi et al.), distinguishes their focus (high-income/low-endemic regions), and explains the need for the current review in LMIC where prior reviews did not assess this population."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_10",
      "quote": "As all included studies were cross-­ sectional, the Joanna Briggs Institute critical appraisal tool for cross-­ sectional studies was used to assess the risk of bias of included studies.",
      "reason": "This review addresses prevalence using cross-sectional studies rather than intervention effects, so describing a mechanism of action for an intervention does not apply."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "The CoCoPop framework was used as this review aims to address a question of prevalence. 26 Due to few existing studies on this topic, the search strategy was intentionally broad with no limits placed on country or year of publication.",
      "reason": "The review is a prevalence (observational) review rather than of complex interventions or delivery contexts, so a logic model for intervention components/outcomes is not applicable."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "85"
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_5",
      "quote": "We aim to address this gap by undertaking a systematic review of the prevalence of HBV amongst refugees, asylum seekers and IDPs in LMIC.",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly states the review objective to undertake a systematic review of HBV prevalence among refugees, asylum seekers and IDPs in LMIC. Additionally, the methods state the use of the CoCoPop framework for a prevalence question (text_7), indicating a relevant question-formulation framework was used."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "The CoCoPop framework was used as this review aims to address a question of prevalence.",
      "reason": "This element applies to reviews of intervention effects requiring PICO. The review addresses prevalence (not interventions), as stated explicitly and by use of the CoCoPop framework, so the PICO requirement is not applicable."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 80
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_8",
      "quote": "Inclusion criteria were articles in English language with full-­ text availability reporting the prevalence of HBV amongst refugees, asylum seekers and/or IDPs currently living in LMIC. LMIC were defined according to World Bank classification. 27 We included studies that measured HBV prevalence using hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg) and/or hepatitis B virus DNA. Given few existing studies on this topic, inclusion criteria were broad and no limits were applied for age, setting (e.g. camp, clinic, hospital, community), sampling method or number of participants. Refugees, asylum seeker or IDP status was determined based on article terminology, or by described characteristics of the population, noting most articles did not include definitions. The primary outcome was HBV prevalence, but this did not need to be the primary outcome of the study to be eligible. Exclusion criteria were case studies, case series, conference abstracts, modelling studies, economic evaluations or studies from high-­ income countries, including amongst people in camps in high-­ income countries or those had been resettled. Review articles were excluded but noted for background and additional references.",
      "reason": "The eligibility text specifies population (refugees/asylum seekers/IDPs in LMIC), outcomes (HBsAg and/or HBV DNA prevalence), settings (camp, clinic, hospital, community) and lack of limits on age/sampling/number. It lists excluded report types. It does not explicitly state eligible study designs in positive terms or comment on follow-up requirements (though follow-up may be irrelevant for prevalence studies), so not all requested study-characteristic details are fully specified."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_8",
      "quote": "Inclusion criteria were articles in English language with full-­ text availability reporting the prevalence of HBV amongst refugees, asylum seekers and/or IDPs currently living in LMIC.",
      "reason": "Report characteristics are specified: language (English) and full-text availability. Additionally, text_7 states \"no limits placed on country or year of publication,\" and excluded types (conference abstracts etc.) are listed, covering publication/report status and year restrictions."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_11",
      "quote": "Reports excluded: n=37 (Wrong study design (conference abstract, editorial, economic study etc.) (n = 25) HBV not outcome (n = 7) Not LMIC ( n = 7) Wrong population/not refugee (n=3)).",
      "reason": "The study flow/exclusion information explicitly distinguishes studies excluded because HBV was not an outcome (i.e. outcome not measured/relevant) from other exclusion reasons, showing the review distinguished exclusion for not measuring the outcome vs other reasons."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_11",
      "quote": "There were 22 studies reporting outcomes amongst refugees, four for IDPs and two for asylum seekers. Study settings ranged from refugee/IDP camp ( n = 16), clinics ( n = 6), hospitals ( n = 4) and detention centres ( n = 2).",
      "reason": "The review specifies groups used in reporting (refugees, IDPs, asylum seekers) and settings, which indicates how studies were grouped for presentation. However, it does not clearly link these groups to pre-specified comparisons defined in the review objectives or describe groups used in each synthesis in relation to review comparisons, so the requirement is only partially met."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "The CoCoPop framework was used as this review aims to address a question of prevalence. 26 Due to few existing studies on this topic, the search strategy was intentionally broad with no limits placed on country or year of publication.",
      "reason": "The authors provide rationale for using broad eligibility (few existing studies) and for not limiting by country/year, which explains lack of restrictions. However, they do not provide rationale for some notable restrictions that are present (e.g. restricting to English language and full-text availability), so the rationale for all notable restrictions is not fully provided."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "75"
}

